<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Concise activity: order-of-operations "story" for a formula, undone in
     reverse with inverse operations, to solve for x by isolation.

     Design notes (not shown to students):
     - Section 1 (linear a*x+b) establishes the core idea with the least
       possible friction: every operation's inverse is obvious, so the
       point is entirely about ORDER - undo the last thing first.
     - Section 2 (rational a/(bx+c)) is the payoff. The surface form of the
       formula makes it look like the last operation is "divide by
       (bx+c)", which tempts students to reach for "multiply by (bx+c)" as
       if bx+c were an ordinary multiplier - it isn't, since x is buried
       inside it. Reframing a/(bx+c) as a * (bx+c)^{-1} restores the same
       story shape as Section 1: the last operation really is "multiply
       by a," preceded by "take the reciprocal," which is its own
       inverse. Watch for students who try to cross-multiply on instinct
       instead of running the story backward - ask them to write out the
       forward story before touching the equation.
     - Kept intentionally short: one worked numeric example and one
       general (letters) derivation per section, then one independent
       practice problem. Meant to run well under a full class period.
     - Lightweight group work (pairs/groups discuss and fill in blanks
       together) but no formal POGIL roles - keep this fast to run. -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-inverse-operations">
  <title>Method of Isolation: Solving by Inverse Operations</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A formula is a set of instructions applied to a number <m>x</m>, <em>in a specific
      order</em>. Solving an equation built from that formula means undoing the instructions
      in reverse order. This is called <term>isolating</term> <m>x</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Work with your group. In this exercise, we will be deliberate in thinking through
      the order of operations. It will be like thinking about the formula as
      a story of operations that act on <m>x</m> in turn.
      For each problem, clearly identify the forward story first before you
      try to solve anything.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-linear-story" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 1: A Straightforward Story</title>

    <task xml:id="task-linear-forward" workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The formula <m>3x+5</m> tells this story: <q>Start with <m>x</m>, multiply by
          <m>3</m>, then add <m>5</m>.</q> Fill in the blank below the same way.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>x \;\xrightarrow{\times\,3}\; \underline{\qquad\phantom{W}\qquad} \;\xrightarrow{+\,5}\; 3x+5</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-linear-undo-numeric" workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          To solve <m>3x+5=17</m>, undo the story in reverse, undoing the last operation first.
          Fill in every blank, including the operation labels on the arrows.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>\begin{array}{c}3x+5\\=\\17\end{array}
          \;\xrightarrow{\underline{\qquad}}\;
          \begin{array}{c}3x\\=\\\underline{\qquad\phantom{W}\qquad}\end{array}
          \;\xrightarrow{\underline{\qquad}}\;
          \begin{array}{c}x\\=\\\underline{\qquad\phantom{W}\qquad}\end{array}</m>
        </p>
        <p>What is <m>x</m>? Check your answer in the original equation.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-linear-undo-general" workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now do the same thing symbolically. Solve <m>ax+b=c</m> for <m>x</m> by undoing the
          story.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>\begin{array}{c}ax+b\\=\\c\end{array}
          \;\xrightarrow{\underline{\qquad}}\;
          \begin{array}{c}ax\\=\\\underline{\qquad\phantom{W}\qquad}\end{array}
          \;\xrightarrow{\underline{\qquad}}\;
          \begin{array}{c}x\\=\\\underline{\qquad\phantom{W}\qquad}\end{array}</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          Why must you undo "multiply by <m>a</m>" <em>last</em>, even though it
          happened <em>first</em> in the forward story?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-linear-practice" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>Solve each equation by writing and then undoing its story. Show both stories.</p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p><m>4x-7=9</m></p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p><m>-2x+1=13</m></p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-rational-story" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 2: Slightly More Complicated</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Now consider <m>\dfrac{6}{2x+1}</m>. It's tempting to say the last step is
        <q>divide by <m>2x+1</m>,</q> but that's not the same kind of operation as
        before. Here <m>x</m> is <em>inside</em> the formula you're dividing <em>by</em>.
        As an action happening to <m>2x+1</m>, it is <em>not</em> actually division.
        Instead, we remember that <em>dividing</em> by a quantity is the same as
        <em>multiplying</em> by its <term>reciprocal</term>. 
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="task-rational-forward" workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Fill in the forward story for <m>\dfrac{6}{2x+1}</m>. (<q>Recip.</q> means
          <q>take the reciprocal.</q>)
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>x \;\xrightarrow{\times\,2}\; \underline{\qquad\phantom{W}\qquad} \;\xrightarrow{+\,1}\;
          \underline{\qquad\phantom{W}\qquad} \;\xrightarrow{\text{recip.}}\; \underline{\qquad\phantom{W}\qquad}
          \;\xrightarrow{\times\,6}\; \dfrac{6}{2x+1}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-rational-key-idea" workspace="0.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The reciprocal action is its own inverse: taking the reciprocal of a reciprocal
          gets you back where you started. Given that fact, what four steps and in what
          order will undo the actions in the story above? For this task, describe them in words. In the <em>next</em> task, you'll use the actions.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-rational-undo-numeric" workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve <m>\dfrac{6}{2x+1}=2</m> by undoing the story, one arrow at a time &#8212;
          both sides together. Undo the outer two actions first to get down to an equation in
          <m>2x+1</m>, then finish it off just like Section 1. Fill in every blank.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>\begin{array}{c}\dfrac{6}{2x+1}\\=\\2\end{array}
          \;\xrightarrow{\underline{\qquad}}\;
          \begin{array}{c}\dfrac{1}{2x+1}\\=\\\underline{\qquad\phantom{W}\qquad}\end{array}
          \;\xrightarrow{\underline{\qquad}}\;
          \begin{array}{c}2x+1\\=\\\underline{\qquad\phantom{W}\qquad}\end{array}</m>
        </p>
        <p>Bring the value you just found down into the equation below.</p>
        <p>
          <m>\begin{array}{c}2x+1\\=\\\underline{\qquad\phantom{W}\qquad}\end{array}
          \;\xrightarrow{\underline{\qquad}}\;
          \begin{array}{c}2x\\=\\\underline{\qquad\phantom{W}\qquad}\end{array}
          \;\xrightarrow{\underline{\qquad}}\;
          \begin{array}{c}x\\=\\\underline{\qquad\phantom{W}\qquad}\end{array}</m>
        </p>
        <p>Check your answer in the original equation.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-rational-practice" workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve <m>\dfrac{12}{3x-2}=4</m> the same way. If your group has time, also solve
          <m>\dfrac{a}{bx+c}=k</m> for <m>x</m> in terms of <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m>, and
          <m>k</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
